sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: shared child workflow of cluster:update, cluster:activate, cluster:deactivate, cluster:node:add and cluster:node:remove, with the steps of route:update. docs/reference/routes.md#change-a-tld-cluster-state-or-cluster-membership.
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the site child is one Ansible phase per Node (tags site), because each placed Swamp step costs about 1.2 s and the child ran 30 in series (47.6 s measured).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · shared by the Cluster commands
  %% kind: route.reconcile
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane V icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as Route Nodes<br/>workload · Router
  participant V as VPN Node<br/>private DNS
  Note over GW: Parent run holds the plan<br/>transitions stored
  %%> What it is:
  %%> - one child of the parent Cluster command's run; it takes every affected Route and reconciles them together
  %%> What the Gateway stored at admission:
  %%> - a pending replacement for each domain change and the old placement for each scope change
  SW->>N: Make a CSR on the Node<br/>openssl req · key stays on Node
  SW->>GW: Sign the CSR<br/>operation:direct:certificate-sign
  %%> Why a call to the Gateway:
  %%> - certificate signing is the one allowed step-to-Gateway call; the Orbit CA stays in the Gateway
  GW-->>SW: Signed certificate<br/>one batch
  opt Cluster-scoped Route
    SW->>N: Allow the Router on LAN<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall
  end
  SW->>N: Install cert, publish site<br/>child route-create-site · Ansible
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - publishes each new site beside the old one on its workload Node and the Router; Caddy reloads after validation
  opt Laravel target
    SW->>N: Write the new APP_URL<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/line
  end
  SW->>N: Probe the site over TLS<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · SNI domain
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the probe checks the hostname and the Orbit CA; an application HTTP error does not fail it, and a failed probe stops the cutover
  opt DNS answer changes
    SW->>V: Move private DNS<br/>records + catalog, then reload
    SW->>SW: Wait the DNS grace<br/>31 s: TTL 30 s + 1 s
    %%> Why once for all Routes:
    %%> - all names move in one step and the grace is paid once; it counts from the stored time of the last move, so a rerun does not skip it
  end
  SW->>N: Withdraw old placement<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file absent
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - removes the old sites, and the certificates and LAN rules the new placement does not need
  alt every step succeeds
    SW-->>GW: Child succeeded<br/>returns to the parent run
  else a step fails
    SW--xGW: Child failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    Note over GW: Read the failed step<br/>cutover or not
    %%> How the Gateway knows the cutover:
    %%> - it derives it from the failed step, as for `route:update`; a step up to the DNS move is before it
    %%> Result before the cutover:
    %%> - the old Routes stay authoritative and the replacements are `failed`; the Gateway restores the Laravel URLs and private DNS; a failed Caddy validation leaves the live file unchanged, removes the candidate and skips reload
    %%> Result after the cutover:
    %%> - a failure in the DNS grace or the withdrawal leaves the new placement authoritative and the old one retiring; a rerun finishes
    %%> On the Nodes:
    %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same parent command again; every step runs again and skips what already matches
  end
